{"id":"random-array-generation","t":"Seeded bootstrap of the mean","tags":["Probability and Statistics","Data Processing"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"bootstrap_means","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"The bootstrap estimates how much a statistic would vary across datasets by resampling the one dataset you have. Each bootstrap replicate draws $n$ indices **with replacement** from the $n$ observations and recomputes the statistic.\n\nWrite `bootstrap_means(x, n_boot, seed)` that returns `(means, se)`.\n\n## Exact procedure (it decides the random numbers, so follow it exactly)\n1. `rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)`\n2. Draw every index in **one** call: `idx = rng.integers(0, n, size=(n_boot, n))`, where `n = len(x)`. Row `b` holds the indices for replicate `b`.\n3. `means[b]` is the mean of `x` at the indices in row `b`.\n4. `se` is the sample standard deviation of `means` with `ddof=1` (the bootstrap standard error).\n\n## Shapes and types\n- `x`: 1-D float array with `n >= 1`. `n_boot >= 2` and `seed` are Python ints.\n- `means`: `float64` array of shape `(n_boot,)`. `se`: a Python `float`.\n- Use NumPy only, with no loop over replicates.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef bootstrap_means(x, n_boot, seed):\n    \"\"\"Return (means (n_boot,), se float) from a seeded bootstrap of the mean.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Use the `Generator` API (`np.random.default_rng`), not the legacy `np.random.seed` / `np.random.randint` functions. They produce different streams.","`rng.integers(low, high, size=...)` excludes `high`, so `rng.integers(0, n, ...)` gives valid indices. Indexing `x` with the whole `(n_boot, n)` index matrix resamples every replicate at once.","`means = x[idx].mean(axis=1)` and `se = means.std(ddof=1)`. Return the standard error as a Python float."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef bootstrap_means(x, n_boot, seed):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    idx = rng.integers(0, len(x), size=(n_boot, len(x)))   # high is exclusive: values 0 .. n-1\n    means = x[idx].mean(axis=1)                            # x[idx] has shape (n_boot, n)\n    return means, float(means.std(ddof=1))\n","ex_md":"## Why it works\n`default_rng(seed)` creates a PCG64 generator whose stream is fully determined by the seed, so the same seed gives the same indices on every machine running the same NumPy version. One call to `integers` with `size=(n_boot, n)` fills the index matrix row by row. Fancy indexing `x[idx]` then gathers an `(n_boot, n)` matrix of resampled values, and one `mean(axis=1)` gives every replicate's statistic.\n\nThe spread of the bootstrap means approximates the standard error of the sample mean, roughly $\\sigma / \\sqrt{n}$. `ddof=1` is the usual choice because the replicates are treated as a sample.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(B \\cdot n)$ time and memory for the index matrix, where $B$ is `n_boot`. For very large $B \\cdot n$, process replicates in chunks.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Mixing APIs. `np.random.seed(0); np.random.randint(...)` uses the legacy Mersenne Twister and gives different numbers.\n- `rng.integers(0, n - 1, ...)` never picks the last element, because `high` is already exclusive.\n- Drawing one replicate per loop iteration with `size=n`. The statement fixes one call; any other sequence of calls is not guaranteed to produce the same stream.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How do you get a 95% percentile bootstrap confidence interval? (`np.percentile(means, [2.5, 97.5])`.)\n- When does the bootstrap fail? (Heavy-tailed data, statistics like the maximum, and dependent data such as time series, which need a block bootstrap.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([1., 2., 3., 4.])\n5\n0","out":"(array([3.  , 1.25, 3.  , 3.25, 3.25]), 0.8477912478906585)"},{"in":"array([10., 10., 10.])\n3\n1","out":"(array([10., 10., 10.]), 0.0)"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../ml-maths-course/index.html#l12-01","t":"Sampling behavior and estimators","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"../ml-toolkit/index.html#l01-07","t":"Linear algebra and random numbers in NumPy","c":"ML Toolkit"},{"href":"../ml-maths-course/index.html#l13-05","t":"Resampling: bootstrap and permutation tests","c":"ML Maths"}],"related":["aggregation-functions","fancy-indexing"],"ntests":7,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 5, 0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [3.0, 1.25, 3.0, 3.25, 3.25], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.8477912478906585]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [10.0, 10.0, 10.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 3, 1], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [10.0, 10.0, 10.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [7.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 4, 2], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [7.0, 7.0, 7.0, 7.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [-5.0, 5.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 6, 3], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.0, -5.0, 0.0, 5.0, -5.0, -5.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 4.08248290463863]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1000000000.0, 1000000001.0, 1000000002.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 4, 4], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [1000000002.0, 1000000001.6666666, 1000000001.0, 1000000000.6666666], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.6085806267050352]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [53.58773408003914, 65.10677308143457, 32.136687626888175, 66.86613508665752, 49.526827878438624, 42.00021415624847, 41.97043281609638, 39.17183483417409, 47.76354641592549, 58.338841795521574, 55.84063731642971, 56.382860242561826, 33.05191608230987, 34.29037882308596, 65.53803174289652, 59.6888536545666, 71.8321400147264, 62.098158348986146, 39.75608664945443, 62.85272454812274, 56.28394264270331, 52.148249276789194, 41.80126677428117, 50.02780221807224, 48.53001298219572, 58.92535111614822, 51.24496943998714, 65.84745914339628, 42.255142467818516, 54.71102559410429], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 200, 42], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [49.27553853216808, 50.8279099560353, 50.948936336698594, 52.4871761900705, 53.23782287791865, 51.99464872612017, 50.21226